WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.340 --> 00:00:05.130
North Korea has built up a reputation for
being one of the strictest and most rigid

00:00:05.130 --> 00:00:07.070
countries in the world.

00:00:07.070 --> 00:00:11.960
From their tight control on emigration to
their unforgiving labor camps, it’s no question

00:00:11.960 --> 00:00:15.120
that North Korea is not messing around.

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:19.580
One of the most notable aspects of North Korea
has been their missile and rocket development

00:00:19.580 --> 00:00:20.580
effort.

00:00:20.580 --> 00:00:25.369
The media has been pointing to this as being
a major threat for decades now, and leaders

00:00:25.369 --> 00:00:29.650
around the world have consistently tried to
get North Korea to denuclearize.

00:00:29.650 --> 00:00:34.130
As the world powers move into space and create
things like the US Space Force, however, will

00:00:34.130 --> 00:00:38.649
North Korea be able to keep up or is nuclear
technology their last hoorah?

00:00:38.649 --> 00:00:44.060
Well, here’s everything we know about North
Korea’s elusive space agency.

00:00:44.060 --> 00:00:48.809
Taking a look back, North Korea didn’t establish
a space program for quite some time after

00:00:48.809 --> 00:00:50.370
World War 2.

00:00:50.370 --> 00:00:55.280
It wasn’t until the 1980s that North Korea
created the Korean Committee of Space Technology

00:00:55.280 --> 00:00:56.940
or the KCST.

00:00:56.940 --> 00:01:02.219
The KCST was originally responsible for everything
related to the country's space effort from

00:01:02.219 --> 00:01:05.579
designing new satellites to developing launch
technology.

00:01:05.579 --> 00:01:09.259
The organization's first step though was building
a launch facility.

00:01:09.259 --> 00:01:15.549
In the early 1980s, the KCST built the Tonghae
Satellite launching ground in the North Hamgyong

00:01:15.549 --> 00:01:16.549
Province.

00:01:16.549 --> 00:01:19.479
The construction effort wasn’t super organized
though.

00:01:19.479 --> 00:01:24.479
The North Koreans built the launch facility
on and off throughout the 1980s and 1990s.

00:01:24.479 --> 00:01:30.270
By 1985, they completed construction of their
launch pad, but aside from that, they didn’t

00:01:30.270 --> 00:01:32.729
have much to show after years of construction.

00:01:32.729 --> 00:01:37.759
They simply had a rudimentary command bunker,
a radar facility, and a few storage and support

00:01:37.759 --> 00:01:38.759
buildings.

00:01:38.759 --> 00:01:42.569
The initial goal was to just reverse engineer
the Soviet Union’s Scud rocket and produce

00:01:42.569 --> 00:01:44.070
their own copies though.

00:01:44.070 --> 00:01:48.079
So, the rudimentary infrastructure was enough
for the time being.

00:01:48.079 --> 00:01:52.770
Throughout the 1990s, however, the North Koreans
would significantly expand this facility.

00:01:52.770 --> 00:01:56.499
Reports suggest that the site grew from just
2 kilometers in length to 9 kilometers in

00:01:56.499 --> 00:01:57.599
length.

00:01:57.599 --> 00:02:02.039
The new additions included a missile assembly
facility, a fuel storage facility, a guidance

00:02:02.039 --> 00:02:05.200
and range control centre, and tracking facilities.

00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:08.789
As the Tonghae Satellite Launching ground
was finished up, the North Koreans shifted

00:02:08.789 --> 00:02:13.450
their effort to building a new launching site
called the Sohae Satellite Launching Station.

00:02:13.450 --> 00:02:17.849
Construction began in the early 1990s, but
like the Tonghae site, construction would

00:02:17.849 --> 00:02:20.329
stretch out for quite some time.

00:02:20.329 --> 00:02:23.790
It wasn’t until 2011 that the site was completed.

00:02:23.790 --> 00:02:28.170
The North Koreans did get a lot more work
done though as the base ended up being 6 square

00:02:28.170 --> 00:02:32.160
kilometers which is 5 times larger than the
Tonghae site.

00:02:32.160 --> 00:02:37.280
Aside from being much larger, the Sohae site
was also far more advanced and included elements

00:02:37.280 --> 00:02:41.590
such as a static rocket motor test stand and
a much more substantial mission control center

00:02:41.590 --> 00:02:44.450
instead of the command bunker they used to
use.

00:02:44.450 --> 00:02:48.990
In terms of actual missions and space programs,
the North Koreans have only had three substantial

00:02:48.990 --> 00:02:52.269
programs thus far with the first one being
Taepodong.

00:02:52.269 --> 00:02:57.000
Taepodong was the final result of trying to
replicate the Soviet’s Scud rocket.

00:02:57.000 --> 00:03:02.129
The Scud rocket was a series of tactical ballistic
missiles, so initially, there were fears that

00:03:02.129 --> 00:03:05.549
North Korea could use Taepodong as a surface
to surface missile.

00:03:05.549 --> 00:03:09.900
But, the US Defense Intelligence Agency would
later suggest that Taepodong was simply a

00:03:09.900 --> 00:03:11.810
test bed for developing more advanced multi-stage
missile technology.

00:03:11.810 --> 00:03:15.990
Anyway, in August of 1998, North Korea launched
Taepodong-1.

00:03:15.990 --> 00:03:21.040
North Korean media reported that Taepodong-1
reached orbit within 5 minutes after launch

00:03:21.040 --> 00:03:24.569
and that they had successfully launched their
first satellite.

00:03:24.569 --> 00:03:28.170
But western scientists disagree with this
report.

00:03:28.170 --> 00:03:32.340
According to their observations, Taepodong-1
was launched and it did reach an altitude

00:03:32.340 --> 00:03:34.000
of 200 kilometers.

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:38.230
However, they suspect that the third stage
failed to ignite as the satellite never actually

00:03:38.230 --> 00:03:39.730
reached orbit.

00:03:39.730 --> 00:03:43.849
Based on where the third stage landed, scientists
estimate that Taepodong could reach up to

00:03:43.849 --> 00:03:47.349
5900 kilometers though if the mission was
successful.

00:03:47.349 --> 00:03:50.489
And that brings us into their second attempt
with Taepodong.

00:03:50.489 --> 00:03:56.739
North Korea launched Taepodong 2 in July of
2006, nearly 8 years after Taepodong 1.

00:03:56.739 --> 00:04:01.569
Unfortunately, there is absolutely no footage
or images relating to this mission, but the

00:04:01.569 --> 00:04:05.499
US Department of Defense did confirm that
the launch did in fact take place.

00:04:05.499 --> 00:04:10.010
There’s not too much to discuss though as
the missile apparently failed just 40 seconds

00:04:10.010 --> 00:04:11.010
after launch.

00:04:11.010 --> 00:04:15.300
Though it doesn’t seem like the reliability
of the missile was improved, observers speculate

00:04:15.300 --> 00:04:17.519
that the missile was much more powerful.

00:04:17.519 --> 00:04:22.380
It’s estimated that each stage of the missile
could have a burn time of about 100 seconds

00:04:22.380 --> 00:04:25.960
which translates to a total burn time of 5
to 6 minutes.

00:04:25.960 --> 00:04:30.240
With a bit of tweaks, this could allow for
up to 9000 kilometers of range with a payload

00:04:30.240 --> 00:04:33.440
capacity of up to 500 kilograms.

00:04:33.440 --> 00:04:37.940
Unfortunately for North Korea though, this
would never materialize, and they would move

00:04:37.940 --> 00:04:40.419
onto a new series of rockets called Unha.

00:04:40.419 --> 00:04:45.370
Unha, which means galaxy, was basically a
frankenstein rocket that incorporated elements

00:04:45.370 --> 00:04:46.820
from a few different rockets.

00:04:46.820 --> 00:04:51.990
The first stage of Unha uses four Nodong motors
which are simply enlarged versions of Scud

00:04:51.990 --> 00:04:52.990
motors.

00:04:52.990 --> 00:04:57.300
Originally, the second stage was thought to
be based on the Soviet SS-N-6 missile.

00:04:57.300 --> 00:05:02.830
But, now the general consensus is that the
second stage is also based on the Scud rocket.

00:05:02.830 --> 00:05:08.340
As for the third and fourth stage, these seem
to be identical to the Iranian Safir rocket’s

00:05:08.340 --> 00:05:09.340
second stage.

00:05:09.340 --> 00:05:13.500
So, once again, it doesn’t seem like North
Korea added much themselves when designing

00:05:13.500 --> 00:05:18.220
the Unha rocket as it's just a combination
of Scud and Safir.

00:05:18.220 --> 00:05:22.319
Despite this, the Unha rocket would stir up
quite a bit of controversy.

00:05:22.319 --> 00:05:27.520
You see, according to the United Nations Security
Council Resolution of 1718, North Korea was

00:05:27.520 --> 00:05:30.140
prohibited from testing ballistic missiles.

00:05:30.140 --> 00:05:33.860
And as you might’ve guessed Unha was a ballistic
missile.

00:05:33.860 --> 00:05:38.480
The US, South Korea, and Japan would all raise
red flags that this launch would violate the

00:05:38.480 --> 00:05:39.830
agreement.

00:05:39.830 --> 00:05:43.180
Even Russia urged North Korea to cancel the
planned launch.

00:05:43.180 --> 00:05:48.371
Nonetheless, North Korea would go ahead and
launch Unha-2 on April 5, 2009 according to

00:05:48.371 --> 00:05:49.729
South Korea.

00:05:49.729 --> 00:05:53.289
The US Northern Command identified that the
first stage of the rocket fell into the Sea

00:05:53.289 --> 00:05:56.780
of Japan and the other stages fell into the
Pacific Ocean.

00:05:56.780 --> 00:06:02.870
The missile traveled a rather impressive 3850
kilometers from the launch site, but the US

00:06:02.870 --> 00:06:07.190
suggests that the third stage didn’t actually
separate and that the rocket failed to insert

00:06:07.190 --> 00:06:09.099
anything into orbit.

00:06:09.099 --> 00:06:13.470
North Korea, however, still claims that the
rocket was fully successful and that it put

00:06:13.470 --> 00:06:15.650
a satellite into orbit.

00:06:15.650 --> 00:06:21.430
Moving onto their second launch of Unha, we
have Unha-3 which took place in April of 2012.

00:06:21.430 --> 00:06:26.370
North Korea was actually super confident about
this launch and they even invited observers

00:06:26.370 --> 00:06:29.440
from eight different countries to come witness
the launch.

00:06:29.440 --> 00:06:33.360
But unfortunately for North Korea, the launch
was a failure.

00:06:33.360 --> 00:06:38.560
We don’t really have any details as to what
exactly happened, but it does seem like North

00:06:38.560 --> 00:06:41.970
Korea was indeed super close to successfully
launching a satellite.

00:06:41.970 --> 00:06:46.990
Just 8 months later in December of 2012, another
Unha-3 rocket was launched.

00:06:46.990 --> 00:06:51.340
And this rocket would successfully launch
a satellite into Sun Synchronous orbit.

00:06:51.340 --> 00:06:55.930
Though this was a massive milestone for the
North Koreans, it wasn’t all good news.

00:06:55.930 --> 00:07:01.000
A few days after the launch, Harvard astronomer
John McDowell suggested that though the satellite

00:07:01.000 --> 00:07:06.199
had reached orbit, it was uncontrollably tumbling
in orbit and the satellite had yet to transmit

00:07:06.199 --> 00:07:07.250
anything.

00:07:07.250 --> 00:07:10.819
Following the partially successful mission,
the North Koreans decided that it was time

00:07:10.819 --> 00:07:11.990
to rebrand themselves.

00:07:11.990 --> 00:07:17.349
In April of 2013, they created a new space
agency called the National Aerospace Development

00:07:17.349 --> 00:07:18.910
Administration or NADA.

00:07:18.910 --> 00:07:24.820
I don’t think North Korea realized that
NADA means nothing in Spanish, but this coincidence

00:07:24.820 --> 00:07:27.300
has become a running joke within the media.

00:07:27.300 --> 00:07:32.780
Anyway, moving onto the final and most recent
launch, we have another Unha-3 launch.

00:07:32.780 --> 00:07:37.729
This final launch took place in February of
2016, and it seems to be North Korea’s first

00:07:37.729 --> 00:07:39.400
fully successful launch.

00:07:39.400 --> 00:07:44.349
The director of the scientific research department
of NADA confirmed that they had received photographs

00:07:44.349 --> 00:07:48.240
from the satellite, and thus far, there has
been no evidence suggesting otherwise.

00:07:48.240 --> 00:07:53.180
So, North Korea does have the capability to
launch small satellites into orbit.

00:07:53.180 --> 00:07:58.120
As for the satellites themselves, all of the
North Korean satellites are part of the Kwangmyŏngsŏng

00:07:58.120 --> 00:07:59.120
program.

00:07:59.120 --> 00:08:03.099
China helped North korea develop the first
Kwangmyŏngsŏng satellite, and it seems like

00:08:03.099 --> 00:08:06.910
North Korea has simply been improving the
Chinese design ever since.

00:08:06.910 --> 00:08:11.069
There have been a total of four satellites
within this program thus far, and two of them

00:08:11.069 --> 00:08:14.199
have made it to orbit with one of them tumbling
around.

00:08:14.199 --> 00:08:18.900
These satellites are simply communication/earth
observation satellites, so they’re thought

00:08:18.900 --> 00:08:20.830
to be completely benign.

00:08:20.830 --> 00:08:25.409
And that’s basically everything we publicly
know about North Korea’s space agency.

00:08:25.409 --> 00:08:29.300
But that still leaves the question, how dangerous
is North Korea’s Space agency?

00:08:29.300 --> 00:08:32.970
Well, the Center for Strategic and International
Studies or CSIS at Georgetown University answered

00:08:32.970 --> 00:08:34.440
just that.

00:08:34.440 --> 00:08:38.710
According to the CSIS, there are four major
threats that could come from North Korea’s

00:08:38.710 --> 00:08:42.750
rocket/missile program with the first being
kinetic physical counterspace weapons and

00:08:42.750 --> 00:08:45.190
more specifically Kinetic ASATs.

00:08:45.190 --> 00:08:50.400
ASAT stands for Anti-satellite weapon, and
as the name suggests, these weapons are used

00:08:50.400 --> 00:08:52.970
to strategically destroy the satellites of
other countries.

00:08:52.970 --> 00:08:58.500
Thus far, North Korea has not tested nor announced
the development of any Kinetic ASAT technology.

00:08:58.500 --> 00:09:03.140
Moreover, even if they wanted to, the CSIS
suggests that North Korea still has several

00:09:03.140 --> 00:09:08.480
technological hurdles to cross to develop
an ASAT, so these aren’t a threat.

00:09:08.480 --> 00:09:12.840
Moving onto the second category, we have Non-Kinetic
Physical Counterspace Weapons.

00:09:12.840 --> 00:09:16.710
One example of such a weapon is launching
a nuclear warhead into space and detonating

00:09:16.710 --> 00:09:17.710
it.

00:09:17.710 --> 00:09:21.520
Some sources suggest that North Korea is actually
working on developing such a device.

00:09:21.520 --> 00:09:26.640
However, there have been no known tests thus
far, and the CSIS doubts that North Korea

00:09:26.640 --> 00:09:29.900
has enough technology to create such a weapon
without help from China or Russia.

00:09:29.900 --> 00:09:34.370
In 2013, a Chinese military commentator actually
suggested that North Korea does possess this

00:09:34.370 --> 00:09:35.370
weapon.

00:09:35.370 --> 00:09:39.450
If this is true, then North Korea likely got
it from China or Russia.

00:09:39.450 --> 00:09:43.330
But given that North Korea can’t yet produce
it themselves, it’s nothing to be worried

00:09:43.330 --> 00:09:44.400
about.

00:09:44.400 --> 00:09:47.320
Moving on, we have electronic counterspace
weapons.

00:09:47.320 --> 00:09:51.810
This includes technology that can jam GPS
and communication signals from satellites.

00:09:51.810 --> 00:09:56.410
North Korea does actually possess this technology,
and they have been using the jamming technology

00:09:56.410 --> 00:09:59.930
against South Korean satellites since August
of 2010.

00:09:59.930 --> 00:10:05.150
This actually violates the 1953 Armistice
agreement, but no action has been taken.

00:10:05.150 --> 00:10:09.320
After all, while jamming signals can be a
nuisance, it’s not necessarily dangerous.

00:10:09.320 --> 00:10:13.590
So, the international community has more or
less just decided to let North Korea have

00:10:13.590 --> 00:10:15.840
a blast jamming satellite signals.

00:10:15.840 --> 00:10:21.160
And finally, the last major space threat from
North Korea is Cyber Counterspace weapons.

00:10:21.160 --> 00:10:25.390
This includes hacking satellites and space
systems from other countries to extract information

00:10:25.390 --> 00:10:27.230
or inject corrupt information.

00:10:27.230 --> 00:10:31.160
The North Korea cyber division is actually
quite strong especially after Kim Jong Un

00:10:31.160 --> 00:10:32.330
took over.

00:10:32.330 --> 00:10:36.810
However, western countries seem to still have
the edge when it comes to cyber warfare.

00:10:36.810 --> 00:10:41.630
So in conclusion, NADA is not that dangerous
at this point in time.

00:10:41.630 --> 00:10:45.450
They’re able to jam signals and might be
able to hack a few satellites.

00:10:45.450 --> 00:10:48.550
But, in terms of physical weapons, it looks
like we're good to go.

00:10:48.550 --> 00:10:49.550
And that’s everything we know about North
Korea’s space agency today.

00:10:49.550 --> 00:10:51.850
Do you guys think North Korea is a serious
threat?

00:10:51.850 --> 00:10:53.090
Comment that down below.

